WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:18.027
BEN: Welcome to People in the News ! Today Olivia is going to tell us about Andrew Lloyd Webber,

2
00:00:18.028 --> 00:00:25.534
a British composer who writes music for musical theater productions. Millions of people around the world have seen his

3
00:00:25.535 --> 00:00:31.366
shows, and he's broken all kinds of box office records. Right, Olivia? OLIVIA: That’s right, Ben. Andrew is

4
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:37.682
one of the most successful composers in the world. Several of his musicals, such as Cats and The Phantom

5
00:00:37.683 --> 00:00:45.102
of the Opera , have been megahits! Andrew was born into a family of musicians on March 22, 1948,

6
00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:52.927
in London, England. His mother, Jean, was a well-known piano teacher. His father, William, was a church organist and

7
00:00:52.928 --> 00:01:00.086
music professor. While Andrew’s brother, Julian, excelled on the cello, Andrew was not the best piano student. He

8
00:01:00.087 --> 00:01:06.316
often preferred to play his own compositions instead of working on his lessons! Andrew started composing at age seven.

9
00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:15.905
His music was first published in a magazine for music teachers when he was only 11 years old. Despite

10
00:01:15.906 --> 00:01:22.817
Andrew’s early signs of talent, his father discouraged him from pursuing a career in music. William wrote church music,

11
00:01:22.818 --> 00:01:29.229
but he had always regretted his lack of success as a classical composer. He told Andrew that music

12
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:34.638
was an awful business, and he should avoid it! Young Andrew liked history, and he was especially interested in

13
00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:41.225
old buildings and monuments. So the Lloyd Webbers often spent their vacations traveling around Great Britain, looking at Roman

14
00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:50.958
ruins and the remains of ancient Welsh castles. Andrew’s Aunt Vi was the one who sparked a love for

15
00:01:50.959 --> 00:02:02.883
musical theater in Andrew. An actress, she often took him to see plays in London’s theater district, called

16
00:02:02.884 --> 00:02:15.039
the West End. Vi also suggested that Andrew build a toy theater to put on shows for his family.

17
00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:21.268
So Andrew constructed an elaborate theater that even had a revolving stage. Andrew played the piano while Julian moved

18
00:02:21.269 --> 00:02:30.436
the toy "actors" around the set. When Andrew was in high school, a young lyricist named Tim Rice heard

19
00:02:30.437 --> 00:02:35.188
that Andrew was looking for someone to write words for his music. So he wrote to Andrew, and

20
00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:48.161
they began to discuss working together. In the meantime Andrew was accepted to Oxford University to study history. Some

21
00:02:48.162 --> 00:02:52.483
people thought he was going to become a historian, but Andrew had another motive for attending Oxford. He’d heard

22
00:02:52.484 --> 00:02:55.242
a lot of good lyricists attended the school. BEN: So did he find a lyricist? OLIVIA: Yes, but not

23
00:02:55.243 --> 00:03:00.484
at Oxford. Andrew didn’t like studying history. After a semester, he left Oxford and moved home to collaborate

24
00:03:00.485 --> 00:03:06.982
with Tim Rice. While Andrew studied at the Royal College of Music, Tim worked at a record company. The

25
00:03:06.983 --> 00:03:15.635
two partners had very different personalities. Andrew was quiet and thoughtful while Tim was outgoing and stylish. It took

26
00:03:15.636 --> 00:03:24.882
them several months to learn how to collaborate well. Their first commission came from a music teacher who needed

27
00:03:24.883 --> 00:03:32.032
something for his school chorus to perform at a concert. Using the Biblical story of Joseph and his

28
00:03:32.033 --> 00:03:40.128
brothers, Tim and Andrew wrote Joseph and the Amazing Technicolor Dreamcoat . It debuted in 1968 before an audience

29
00:03:40.129 --> 00:03:49.019
of school parents. At that point it was just 15 minutes long. A music critic for a major London

30
00:03:49.020 --> 00:04:01.849
newspaper attended the next performance at a church. He wrote a favorable review and suddenly all of London was

31
00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:13.418
talking about this talented musical duo! Tim and Andrew soon wrote enough music for a full-length version of

32
00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:18.749
Joseph and the Amazing Technicolor Dreamcoat . Fourteen years later that show was produced on Broadway. In the meantime

33
00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:23.747
Andrew and Tim were busy writing several other musicals. Their first real megahit was the rock opera Jesus Christ

34
00:04:23.748 --> 00:04:32.494
Superstar . By the time Andrew was 25, this show had been produced on Broadway and in the West

35
00:04:32.495 --> 00:04:42.567
End. It was also made into a movie. The songwriters' next big hit came in 1978 with Evita

36
00:04:42.568 --> 00:04:50.227
, based on the life of Eva Peron, wife of an Argentinean dictator. Shortly after this success, Tim and

37
00:04:50.228 --> 00:04:59.705
Andrew decided to end their partnership and work with other songwriters. Andrew's show Cats , based on poetry by

38
00:04:59.706 --> 00:05:06.634
T.S. Eliot, opened in London in 1981 and was an instant success. For a while the show held the

39
00:05:06.635 --> 00:05:09.122
record for the longest-running musical in both London and New York. BEN: What musical broke the record? OLIVIA:

40
00:05:09.123 --> 00:05:16.216
The record was finally broken in New York by one of Andrew's own shows— The Phantom of the Opera

41
00:05:16.217 --> 00:05:24.628
! Phantom opened in London in 1986 and New York in 1988, and it’s still running in both cities.

42
00:05:24.629 --> 00:05:34.187
Over 140 million people have seen the show in thirty countries. It has won more than seventy major theatrical

43
00:05:34.188 --> 00:05:42.012
awards. Andrew himself has won many awards, including seven Tonys, an Oscar, and three Grammys. His musicals often

44
00:05:42.013 --> 00:05:53.430
reflect his love of history and buildings, and he is known for combining different musical forms such as rock

45
00:05:53.431 --> 00:05:59.579
and opera in unusual ways. One of his shows, Starlight Express , even has actors performing on roller skates!

46
00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:07.841
In 1992 he was knighted by Queen Elizabeth II, a sign of his huge success in England. Still, Andrew

47
00:06:07.842 --> 00:06:12.815
has received plenty of criticism during his career. Some religious groups don't like his portrayal of Jesus in

48
00:06:12.816 --> 00:06:24.397
Jesus Christ Superstar . Other people don't think the subject of Evita —a dictator’s wife—is suitable for a musical.

49
00:06:24.398 --> 00:06:28.491
And some professional critics just don’t like his music. But the public certainly seems to enjoy Andrew’s music. While

50
00:06:28.492 --> 00:06:38.231
not all of his shows have been successes, he’s often had several shows running at once. And more than

51
00:06:38.232 --> 00:06:45.635
seven hundred kids showed up to audition for his 2015 Broadway show: School of Rock ! BEN: Thanks,

52
00:06:45.636 --> 00:06:52.475
Olivia. I'm really glad that Andrew didn't listen to his father's advice about avoiding a career in music. Cats

53
00:06:52.476 --> 00:07:02.592
and The Phantom of the Opera are two of my favorite musicals! See you next week, Little Fox readers!

